<h1>Plex Integration — Technical Notes</h1>

<p>
  This note documents how playback progress and watch state move between the Nullcast
  client and the Plex Media Server, and — specifically — <strong>who decides</strong> what
  counts as "watched" when you request a category like <em>Never Watched</em>.
</p>

<h2>The short version</h2>

<p>
  <strong>Plex is the source of truth for all watch state, not the client.</strong> The
  client emits playback <em>events</em> and reads back the <em>results</em>; the Plex server
  owns the watched/unwatched decision, stores it in its own database, and executes category
  filtering. If you played the same library in the official Plex app, the two would agree,
  because both write to and read from the same server-side state.
</p>

<h2>1. Progress reporting (client → server)</h2>

<p>
  During playback the client continuously reports position to Plex's timeline endpoint. This
  is the mechanism through which Plex learns what you have watched. There is
  <strong>no explicit "mark watched"</strong> call — no <code>/:/scrobble</code> or
  <code>/:/unscrobble</code> — the client only pushes timeline progress and lets the server
  infer completion.
</p>

<p>Implemented in <code>ReportTimelineAsync</code>, <code>app-flyleaf/Services/PlexService.cs:388-406</code>:</p>

<pre><code class="language-csharp">var url =
    $"{_store.PlexBaseUrl}/:/timeline" +
    $"?ratingKey={Uri.EscapeDataString(item.RatingKey)}" +
    $"&key={key}" +
    $"&state={Uri.EscapeDataString(state)}" +
    $"&time={timeMs}" +
    $"&duration={item.DurationMs}" +
    $"&X-Plex-Token={Uri.EscapeDataString(token)}";
await _http.GetAsync(url);</code></pre>

<p>
  <code>state</code> is one of <code>playing</code>, <code>paused</code>, or
  <code>stopped</code>. The report fires from roughly ten call sites in
  <code>app-flyleaf/MainWindow.xaml.cs</code>:
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>When</th><th>State reported</th><th>Location</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Opening a video</td><td><code>playing</code> (at resume offset)</td><td><code>MainWindow.xaml.cs:1410</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Heartbeat during playback (~1s)</td><td><code>playing</code></td><td><code>MainWindow.xaml.cs:1646</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>User pauses</td><td><code>paused</code></td><td><code>MainWindow.xaml.cs:1901</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>User resumes</td><td><code>playing</code></td><td><code>MainWindow.xaml.cs:1907</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Video ends</td><td><code>stopped</code> (final duration)</td><td><code>MainWindow.xaml.cs:409</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Switching items mid-play</td><td><code>stopped</code></td><td><code>MainWindow.xaml.cs:1383</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Window closing while playing</td><td><code>stopped</code></td><td><code>MainWindow.xaml.cs:1968</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>2. Reading watch state (server → client)</h2>

<p>
  On each browse or metadata fetch the client reads Plex's watch-state fields back out of the
  response and maps them onto its view model. Field definitions live in
  <code>app-flyleaf/Models/PlexItem.cs:48-73</code>:
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>viewOffset</code></td><td>Resume position (ms)</td><td>Resume badge, e.g. <samp>▶ 12:03</samp></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>viewCount</code></td><td>Times finished (server increments)</td><td>Watched vs. unwatched</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>lastViewedAt</code></td><td>Epoch of last view</td><td>"Recently Watched" sort/filter</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>viewedLeafCount</code></td><td>Episodes watched (shows)</td><td>Progress, e.g. <samp>3/62 watched</samp></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>3. Category filtering — who decides "Never Watched"</h2>

<p>
  <strong>The Plex server decides.</strong> Selecting <em>Never Watched</em> appends
  <code>&amp;unwatched=1</code> to the library query; the server filters against its own
  <code>viewCount</code> records and returns only unwatched items. The client never computes
  watched-vs-unwatched itself.
</p>

<p><code>BrowseAsync</code>, <code>app-flyleaf/Services/PlexService.cs:303-309</code>:</p>

<pre><code class="language-csharp">if (view == PlexBrowseView.NeverWatched)
    extra += "&unwatched=1";

var url = $"{_store.PlexBaseUrl}/library/sections/{Uri.EscapeDataString(sectionKey)}/all" +
          $"?type={typeNum}&sort={sort}{extra}&includeGenres=1" +
          $"&X-Plex-Token={Uri.EscapeDataString(_store.GetPlexToken())}";</code></pre>

<p>
  The one exception is <em>Recently Watched</em>, which is filtered <strong>client-side</strong>:
  the server returns items sorted by <code>lastViewedAt</code> and the client drops any with
  <code>lastViewedAt &lt;= 0</code> (<code>app-flyleaf/Services/PlexService.cs:317</code>).
  <em>Never Watched</em> is fully server-side.
</p>

<h2>Division of responsibility</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Concern</th><th>Owner</th><th>How</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Progress tracking</td><td>Client</td><td>Pushes timeline heartbeat with <code>time</code>/<code>duration</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>The watched/unwatched <em>decision</em></td><td>Plex server</td><td>Threshold logic (~90% of duration) + its own DB; bumps <code>viewCount</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Category filtering (<em>Never Watched</em>)</td><td>Plex server</td><td>Executes <code>unwatched=1</code> against its records</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Resume position display</td><td>Client</td><td>Reads back <code>viewOffset</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Practical implication</h2>

<p>
  Because watched status depends entirely on Plex receiving progress reports, a video only
  becomes "watched" once the server has seen a <code>stopped</code> report (or enough
  heartbeats to cross its completion threshold). The client reports <code>stopped</code> on
  graceful window close (<code>app-flyleaf/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1968</code>), which covers the
  normal path. A hard kill mid-playback that skips the closing report can leave Plex with a
  stale <code>viewOffset</code> and an un-incremented <code>viewCount</code> — the item stays
  "in progress" rather than "watched."
</p>

<h2>Endpoints used</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Location</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>/:/timeline</code></td><td>Report playback progress</td><td><code>PlexService.cs:396</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/library/sections/{id}/all</code></td><td>Browse library (with <code>unwatched=1</code>)</td><td><code>PlexService.cs:307</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/library/metadata/{id}</code></td><td>Fetch single item</td><td><code>PlexService.cs:187, 206</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/library/metadata/{id}/children</code></td><td>Drill down (show → season → episode)</td><td><code>PlexService.cs:337</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/library/sections</code></td><td>List video libraries</td><td><code>PlexService.cs:233</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/hubs/search</code></td><td>Search</td><td><code>PlexService.cs:137</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/identity</code></td><td>Connection test</td><td><code>PlexService.cs:89</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><small>Authentication on every call is the <code>X-Plex-Token</code> query parameter, decrypted on demand via <code>_store.GetPlexToken()</code>.</small></p>
